    "text": "Two radioactive substances <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>X</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Y</mi></math> originally have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> nuclei respectively. Half life of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>X</mi></math> is half of the half life of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Y</mi></math>. After three half lives of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Y</mi></math>, number of nuclei of both are equal. The ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></math> will be equal to :",
